WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:05.740
The breadboard workspace lets you connect a circuit diagram to a physical-style layout.

2
00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:10.159
We will use the ten volt divider with two ten kilo-ohm resistors.

3
00:00:10.409 --> 00:00:12.577
Open Breadboard from the top bar.

4
00:00:12.827 --> 00:00:16.276
To practice from the beginning. choose Start over. practice.

5
00:00:16.526 --> 00:00:21.123
The intended circuit remains available while the component assembly is cleared.

6
00:00:22.923 --> 00:00:28.085
The left panel separates parts required by the current circuit from the wider Parts kit.

7
00:00:28.335 --> 00:00:32.409
Start with This circuit so the references and values match the schematic.

8
00:00:32.659 --> 00:00:35.126
Pick a component and place it on the board.

9
00:00:35.376 --> 00:00:40.980
Pay attention to which holes share a conducting strip; visual closeness alone does not

10
00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:42.180
determine the connection.

11
00:00:43.981 --> 00:00:45.181
Choose Guide me.

12
00:00:45.431 --> 00:00:48.737
The current step names the component and its target holes.

13
00:00:48.987 --> 00:00:54.182
The matching part is highlighted in the left panel. and markers identify the locations on

14
00:00:54.182 --> 00:00:54.874
the board.

15
00:00:55.124 --> 00:00:57.920
Read the explanation before placing the part.

16
00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:02.989
A resistor must span different electrical nodes. rather than have both leads shorted

17
00:01:02.989 --> 00:01:04.472
together on one strip.

18
00:01:06.272 --> 00:01:10.474
Show placement gives an optional visual preview of where the part goes.

19
00:01:10.724 --> 00:01:13.810
You can also enable automatic previews for the steps.

20
00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:18.422
The preview helps you plan; it does not mean you have completed the construction.

21
00:01:18.672 --> 00:01:24.103
Place the part yourself. or use Do this step when you want the software to perform one

22
00:01:24.103 --> 00:01:24.742
guided placement.

23
00:01:26.542 --> 00:01:31.394
After the first resistor is placed. inspect both leads and the strip connections.

24
00:01:31.644 --> 00:01:35.120
Continue through the remaining components and jumper wires.

25
00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:37.197
Keep power off while assembling.

26
00:01:37.447 --> 00:01:43.291
If you need to reposition a part. select it and use the move and rotation controls. then

27
00:01:43.291 --> 00:01:45.010
check its terminal locations again.

28
00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:49.217
Do this step places the component for you and counts as assistance.

29
00:01:49.467 --> 00:01:52.890
For an independent assessed build. place the parts yourself.

30
00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:58.593
A red Doctor indicator means there are findings to inspect.

31
00:01:58.843 --> 00:02:04.681
Open it to see specific issues. such as an unconnected source lead or an incomplete return

32
00:02:04.681 --> 00:02:05.046
path.

33
00:02:05.296 --> 00:02:09.604
Show me locates the relevant area. and How to fix it explains the problem.

34
00:02:09.854 --> 00:02:15.379
Use the evidence to correct the wiring; a flag during an unfinished build is expected.

35
00:02:17.179 --> 00:02:20.199
Build for me assembles a complete reference build.

36
00:02:20.449 --> 00:02:24.395
Use it when you want to compare layouts or recover a working example.

37
00:02:24.645 --> 00:02:27.972
It serves a different purpose from building the circuit yourself.

38
00:02:28.222 --> 00:02:32.996
Here. the reference construction is complete. and Doctor reports no problems.

39
00:02:33.246 --> 00:02:35.639
Check the result before switching power on.

40
00:02:37.439 --> 00:02:41.311
With the completed divider powered on. set the meter to voltage.

41
00:02:41.561 --> 00:02:46.029
Place the red probe at the resistor midpoint and the black probe at the return.

42
00:02:46.279 --> 00:02:50.321
The meter reads five volts. matching the equal-resistor prediction.

43
00:02:50.571 --> 00:02:56.335
Always identify both probe locations: a voltage measurement is a difference between two

44
00:02:56.335 --> 00:02:56.779
points.

45
00:02:58.579 --> 00:03:03.260
When you are ready for another attempt. turn power off and use Start over. practice.

46
00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:07.797
Rebuild without the completed reference. then check and measure your result.

47
00:03:08.047 --> 00:03:13.093
Save the learning session when you want to keep your circuit. breadboard work. notes. and

48
00:03:13.093 --> 00:03:13.430
progress.

49
00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:18.873
The next tutorial adds the generator. oscilloscope. and frequency-response tools.

50
00:03:19.123 --> 00:03:23.513
In a course breadboard assignment. use Build. Check. Measure. and Principles.

51
00:03:23.763 --> 00:03:25.964
No written prediction is required.

52
00:03:26.214 --> 00:03:31.662
Save a valid powered measurement. explain the circuit principle. and submit the activity

53
00:03:31.662 --> 00:03:32.081
locally.

54
00:03:32.331 --> 00:03:34.892
Download your progress separately for backup.
